iv><p>The source example shows one way noun forms can contribute to a <strong class="key">formal, compact style</strong>. However, students should not nominalise every verb automatically; <strong>clarity remains the priority</strong>.</p><textarea placeholder="Rewrite: The motor became unstable because the load increased."></textarea><div class="student-answer-note">Answer key: reviewed in class.</div></div></div><div class="quiz" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="q"><b>1. Which sentence gives the strongest academic report of controller performance?</b><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="a"/> The proposed controller <strong>significantly improved system performance</strong>.</label><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="b"/> Under the tested load conditions, the proposed controller reduced <strong>THD from 8.4% to 2.1%</strong>.</label><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="c"/> The proposed controller produced <strong>better harmonic performance</strong> than the baseline.</label><div class="fb"></div></div><div class="q"><b>2. Which sentence is more precise?</b><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="a"/> Current increased from <strong>1.2 A to 1.8 A over 4 seconds</strong>.</label><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="b"/> Current increased <strong>substantially over a short period</strong>.</label><div class="fb"></div></div><div class="student-answer-note">Quiz answers will be checked in class.</div></div></section><section><div class="note"><strong>60–70 minutes · 10-MINUTE REST</strong></div></section><section id="precision"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">70–83 minutes</span><h2>Precision, Clarity &amp; Cautious Claims</h2></div><p>Strong technical writing uses <strong>observable, measurable information</strong> and avoids claims that are stronger than the evidence.</p></div><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Use Exact Information</h3><ul><li><strong>3.3 V</strong> rather than <em>low voltage</em>.</li><li><strong>12 ms</strong> rather than <em>very fast</em>.</li><li><strong>83%</strong> rather than <em>many participants</em>.</li><li><strong>10 cm²</strong> rather than <em>small</em>.</li></ul><div class="takeaway"><strong>Key point:</strong> include a <strong>number + unit + comparison or condition</strong> whenever it genuinely improves meaning.</div></div><div class="card"><h3>Use Accurate Technical Vocabulary</h3><p>Choose the <strong class="key">most accurate term</strong> rather than a rare synonym merely to sound sophisticated.</p><div class="micro-list"><div class="micro"><strong>Prefer:</strong><em>voltage fluctuation</em> when that is the technical phenomenon.</div><div class="micro"><strong>Avoid:</strong> replacing a correct engineering term with an approximate “advanced” synonym.</div><div class="micro"><strong>Define:</strong> unfamiliar abbreviations and specialist terms when first introduced.</div></div></div></div><div class="card" style="margin-top:14px"><h3>Cautious Academic Claims — Hedging</h3><p>Objectivity also means matching the <strong>strength of your language</strong> to the <strong>strength of the evidence</strong>.</p><div class="bad">The results <strong>show that X causes Y</strong>.</div><div class="good" style="margin-top:8px">The results <strong>suggest</strong> that X <strong>contributes to</strong> Y.</div><div class="hedge-tools"><span>may</span><span>appears to</span><span>is likely to</span><span>tends to</span><span>approximately</span><span>these findings indicate</span></div><div class="takeaway" style="margin-top:12px"><span class="tag">Question</span><strong>What evidence would justify “causes”?</strong> Then contrast it with <strong>suggests</strong> or <strong>is associated with</strong>.</div></div><div class="task" style="margin-top:14px"><h3>Activity C — Make It Precise</h3><p>Rewrite each sentence using <strong>quantification</strong>, <strong>units</strong>, a <strong>condition</strong>, or a <strong>measured comparison</strong>.</p><div class="prompt">The temperature became <strong>very high</strong>.</div><textarea placeholder="Rewrite with an exact value or range..."></textarea><div class="prompt">The battery lasted <strong>a long time</strong>.</div><textarea placeholder="Rewrite with duration and test condition..."></textarea><div class="prompt">The response was <strong>much faster than before</strong>.</div><textarea placeholder="Rewrite with a measured comparison..."></textarea><div class="student-answer-note">Answer key: reviewed in class.</div></div></section><section id="real-paper"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">Real-paper language lab · use within 70–83 minutes</span><h2>Academic English in a Real Electrical Engineering Paper</h2></div><p>This example is used for <strong>language analysis only</strong>: students observe how published engineers express purpose, caution, technical precision and evidence.</p></div><div class="card" style="border-top:6px solid var(--gold)"><h3>Open-access paper</h3><p><strong>Xiu-Hua Guo, Che-Wei Chang &amp; Le-Ren Chang-Chien (2022)</strong><br/><em>Digital Implementation of Harmonic and Unbalanced Load Compensation for Voltage Source Inverter to Operate in Grid Forming Microgrid</em>, <strong>Electronics, 11(6), 886</strong>.</p><p><strong>Electrical Engineering context:</strong> voltage-source inverter (VSI), grid-forming microgrid, FFT, proportional-resonant control, total harmonic distortion (THD), predictive current control, and experimental verification.</p><div class="actions" style="margin-top:10px"><a class="btn alt" href="https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/11/6/886" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open full paper →</a><a class="btn" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics11060886" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Open DOI →</a></div></div><div class="table-scroll" style="margin-top:14px"><table><tr><th>Short phrase from the paper</th><th>Language feature</th><th>What to notice</th></tr><tr><td>“<u class="paper-phrase">is presented in this paper</u>”</td><td><strong>Formal purpose / reporting language</strong></td><td>The writer states what the paper introduces without conversational wording.</td></tr><tr><td>“<u class="paper-phrase">could be reduced</u>”</td><td><strong>Hedging + passive voice</strong></td><td><strong>Could</strong> makes the claim more cautious; the passive keeps attention on the technical effect.</td></tr><tr><td>“<u class="paper-phrase">to mitigate the total harmonic distor